   "text": {
    "L1": "When we observe nature, we look closely and record useful features.",
    "L2": "A body part's shape can be linked to how an animal lives. That idea is true, not a myth.",
    "L3": "A parrot's hooked beak helps it crack nuts and hard seeds and bite fruit.",
    "L4": "A kingfisher has a long, pointed beak for catching fish. A sparrow's short, cone-shaped beak suits seeds.",
    "L5": "Feet also suit a bird's life: webbed feet help swimming, while claws help gripping or scratching.",
    "L6": "Plants suit their homes too. A cactus has spines and a thick stem, a pine has needle leaves, and a lotus has broad waxy leaves.",
    "L7": "Plants are living things because they grow, need water and air, make food, and make new plants.",
    "L8": "On a nature walk, record the feature you observe closely, then link it to how the organism lives."
   },
